emy force and when you're attacking  the final entrance into the main hall of  a fortress as leader feels as reverent  and grand as walking into a towering  Cathedral and your friends let's see  whether you're worthy though it feels  like you don't get to spend quite enough  time with anyone individually except for  a golem whose brief appearance is still  too long the narrative gets muddled by  the lack of a clear overarching goal  outside of fight against saurons forces  in Mordor shadow of wars map once you  get used to the ridiculous number of  icons so would you wear everything you  need to find is but after you've spent a  couple hours running around collecting  artifacts it makes the characters  intreaty about the approaching threat of  the Nazgul and another quest feel a  little hollow the narrative doesn't pull  you along a meaningful path but the  things you do on that journey are  breathlessly fun you write a Drake  team-up with some ridiculous orcs meet  sexy Shelob kill countless more orcs  fight the ring wraiths it's like a  greatest hits compilation of the most  badass moments from The Lord of the  Rings after a slow building introductory  act the game just gains momentum as it  crashes toward a final standoff against  the forces of evil and this battle  addresses criticisms of the previous  game it's an epic multistage fight that  does still have QTEs but no more than  the ones you find while playing through  the game normally  oh the things I would have done to you  but that battle isn't the end of the  game bafflingly shadow of war continues  on its momentum drained by what feels  like an epic climax that descends into a  boring slog in the game's final act you  cycle through the fortresses you  explored previously for a total of  twenty siege battles that took me about  15 to 20 hours to get through the story  stops pulling you along and there are no  narrative characters to keep you company  or explain why it's important to tackle  the defense missions in the order you do  it's an entire section that feels like  it should have been cut along with a few  other new systems that shadow of war to  varying degrees of success tries to  incorporate  you must be the fool who killed my  bodyguards the blood of manse why with  loads from our belly today you know I'll  greet yours first like almost every item  in orc in the game has some type of  Associated rarity and with the higher  rarity comes more abilities for works  that means they have additional more  powerful attributes that aren't  available to common orcs for weapons  that includes relatively minor perks  like a slight chance of lighting an  enemy on fire but the effects aren't so  amazing that you'd keep a significantly  underpowered weapon or work just for its  benefits you earn new weapons and new  orcs at such a pace that you're  constantly swapping out old gear for new  and all of those abilities are wrapped  in complicated text heavy menus and  speaking of convoluted menu systems  there's shadow of wars skill system each  main skill has a separate unlockable set  of 2 to 3 sub skills the ability grid is  so dense and spread out that it's a  chore just to read through and decide  what to put your points into and  reallocating in the middle of battle say  if you've won an area of effect attack  that shoot out flames instead of poison  involves far too many nested menus to  ever be practical but despite the  bloated feel of these systems you earn  all of these skill points weapons and  orcs it such a frantic pace that weirdly  the game doesn't feel dragged down in  the same way as it does by that final  act shadow of war is at its core a great  experience that's marred by a completely  unnecessary skippable conclusion but  that's illustrative of the game as a  whole it tries to be larger than its  predecessor but that leads to its  biggest problems there are more  abilities more weapons more orcs but  left me wanting less a more refined  skill system fe